? On a Cortex-M NVIC, which interrupt is more urgent?
* The one with the lower priority number
- The one with the higher priority number
- The one with the lower IRQ number, regardless of priority
- The one that was enabled first
> Priority 0 is the most urgent configurable level. Ties between equal priorities go to the lower exception number.

? An interrupt of the same priority as the running handler becomes pending. What happens?
- It preempts the running handler
* It waits until the running handler returns, then runs (tail-chaining)
- It is lost
- The CPU raises a HardFault
> Preemption requires strictly higher urgency.

? What does setting BASEPRI to a non-zero value do?
- Masks every interrupt, like PRIMASK
* Masks interrupts whose priority value is >= BASEPRI, leaving more urgent ones enabled
- Raises the priority of the running handler
- Disables the NVIC
> RTIC uses BASEPRI to implement resource locks without blocking unrelated higher-priority tasks.

? Why is `if no_events() { wfi() }` racy, and how is it fixed?
- It isn't racy
* An interrupt can arrive between the check and `wfi`, so the CPU sleeps with work pending; fix by disabling interrupts, checking, `wfi` (which still wakes on pending interrupts), then re-enabling
- `wfi` can't be used in Rust
- Use a spinlock around `wfi`
> `wfi` wakes on pending interrupts even when PRIMASK masks them, which makes the disable-check-sleep-enable sequence correct.

? What must an interrupt handler usually do before returning?
- Re-enable all interrupts
* Clear the peripheral's interrupt flag (or read/write the register that does), or it re-enters immediately
- Call the executor
- Allocate a buffer for the next event
> Most peripherals keep the IRQ line asserted until the cause is acknowledged.

? What is the job of `Waker::wake`?
- Run the task immediately on the current stack
* Tell the executor the task can make progress, so it should be polled again
- Cancel the future
- Allocate a new task
> Executors decide when to poll; wakers just mark tasks ready (e.g. set a bit, push to a queue).

? What happens to an in-flight future when `select` completes with the other branch?
* It's dropped (cancelled) at its current `.await` point
- It keeps running in the background
- It is restarted from the beginning next time
- It panics
> Cancellation by drop means code must remain correct if stopped at any await point.

? Why are Embassy tasks declared with `#[embassy_executor::task]` statically sized?
- To make them faster
* So task storage is reserved at compile time: no heap, and running out of tasks is detected at spawn time
- Because async closures aren't supported
- So they can run on multiple cores
> The future's state machine size is known at compile time, and Embassy places it in a static arena.

? How does the priority-ceiling protocol (RTIC, AUTOSAR OS resources) prevent priority inversion?
- By disabling all interrupts while any resource is held
* A task holding a resource runs at the highest priority of any task that uses it, so no medium-priority task can preempt it while a high-priority task waits
- By giving each task its own copy of the resource
- By using spinlocks
> It also makes deadlock impossible, and blocking time is bounded by the longest critical section.

? Which of these is in `core`?
- An executor
- `embassy_time::Timer`
* `Future`, `Poll`, `Context`, `Waker`, `RawWakerVTable` and `Waker::noop`
- `tokio::spawn`
> core provides the async plumbing; executors come from crates (Embassy, RTIC's async tasks) or your own code.
